  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nRest of Today through Tuesday...Cold air advection will be the \nmain story for the rest of today into tomorrow as a highs moves \ndown from Canada into the Upper Midwest. This high will continue\nto bring in colder air until it is overhead late Monday. This \nwill have us bottoming out with highs on Monday in the upper 20s\nto lower 30s and lows Monday night in the teens. The normals \nthis time of year are highs in the upper 30s and lows in the mid\n20s. As the high moves east on Tuesday warmer air will begin to\nadvect in and start the gradual warm up for the rest of the \nweek. This air is not much warmer so temperatures on Tuesday \nwill only be a few degrees warmer.\n\nWednesday to Next Weekend... The main feature for the middle of\nthe week will be a shortwave trough moving along a clipper like\npath. It will start in Alberta early in the week before digging\ndeep into the Midwest midweek as shown in the global \ndeterministic models. This seems likely to drive far enough \nsouth that the main precipitation chances will miss us. If there\nwere to be any chance it would be closer to the wave across \nparts of southwest Minnesota on Wednesday. ENS members have the\nmost confidence in this with much less in the GEPS and GEFS. \nEven with the ENS this would be very light QPF, but could be the\nfirst snow if it occurs. This shortwave looks into interact \nwith another wave from near the Gulf Coast in such a way that in\nlingers in the central CONUS for a while before moving east \nlate week. Outside of the previously mentioned Wednesday chance \nin southwest Minnesota, chances for precipitation are low as \nall the forcing and moisture for this system look to remain well\nto our east. Our biggest impact would likely be for those who \nplan to travel on or after the Thanksgiving Holiday. As this \ncould impact areas like Chicago and eastern WI to the east which\ncould hit multiple major airport hubs. Locally we most likely \nstay dry and cool. There is agreement in the global \ndeterministic models for a low moving across southern Canada \nnext weekend. What is less certain in these models and their \nassociated ensemble systems is how far south impacts could \nreach. Still lots of variability here, but this would be the \nnext chance for seeing precipitation. The rain snow line looks \nlike it would be nearby, so this would be a chance for snow as \nwell. MSP already has the latest trace snow for the season set, \nbut how close we get for the latest first 0.1\" (currently Dec 3,\n1928) is still to be decided. If we do see snow it would be \nlight, so right now it looks more likely than not our chances \nfor setting the record will continue.",
